# Notifications Package Testing

Use this rule when adding or changing Vitest specs under
`packages/notifications`.

## Vitest resolve aliases

- Keep `packages/notifications/vitest.config.ts` as the single place that
  maps workspace packages to sibling source entry points.
- Use `resolve.alias` so `@lowroute/config` and `@lowroute/domain` resolve
  to `../config/src/index.ts` and `../domain/src/index.ts` (via
  `fileURLToPath` and `import.meta.url`).
- Goal: `pnpm --filter @lowroute/notifications test` runs without building
  `config` or `domain` first.

## Env and `buildTelegramNotifier`

- Construct a full `Env` object with `readEnv` from a plain object shaped
  like `NodeJS.ProcessEnv`, not from mutable `process.env` unless you reset
  it after each test.
- Pass that object as `options.env` into `buildTelegramNotifier` so behavior
  is explicit and isolated.
- Remember defaults from `readEnv`: `TELEGRAM_ALERTS_ENABLED` is `false`,
  `ALERT_DRY_RUN` is `true`, and empty strings are valid for token and chat
  id fields when you only exercise dry-run paths.

## Mocking `fetch`

- Save `const originalFetch = globalThis.fetch` in `beforeEach` or at
  module scope, assign a `vi.fn` (or stub) to `globalThis.fetch`, and
  restore the original in `afterEach` so other tests and Vitest workers do
  not inherit a broken global.
- For the live-send branch, return a `Response` with controlled `ok`,
  `status`, and `text()` so warning logs and retry loops are deterministic.

## Dry run and kill switch

- When `env.ALERT_DRY_RUN` is true or `env.TELEGRAM_ALERTS_ENABLED` is false,
  `sendDealAlert` must log via the injected logger and return `false`
  without calling `fetch`.
- Specs should assert logger calls and absence of `fetch` when exercising
  those paths.

## Live send path and env

- Live sends require `TELEGRAM_ALERTS_ENABLED: true`, `ALERT_DRY_RUN: false`,
  non-empty `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN`, and `TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID` consistent with
  real Telegram usage only in explicit integration or manual checks; unit
  tests should keep the global `fetch` mocked.

## Retries, attempts, and logs

- Default `retryCount` is `2`, so the implementation uses attempts `0`
  through `retryCount` inclusive (three tries when default).
- On non-2xx responses, the code logs `attempt`, `status`, and
  `responseText`; on thrown errors it logs `attempt` and `error`.
- Tests can drive failure then success to confirm the loop and final return
  value (`true` only after `response.ok`).

## Integration points

- `readEnv` lives in `@lowroute/config`; types and fixtures for offers come
  from `@lowroute/domain`.
- `buildTelegramNotifier` is the public factory; prefer testing through it
  rather than reaching into private formatters unless a unit is exported.

## Pitfalls

- Omitting Vitest aliases causes resolution against package `main`/`types`
  (`dist`), which fails if dependencies are not built.
- Mutating `process.env` without cleanup leaks settings across files when
  Vitest runs tests in parallel.
- Forgetting to restore `globalThis.fetch` breaks unrelated specs.
- Assuming two attempts when asserting call counts; match the inclusive
  `attempt <= retryCount` loop.

## Reference implementation

- Alias wiring: `packages/notifications/vitest.config.ts`
- Notifier behavior: `packages/notifications/src/telegram/telegram-notifier.ts`
